---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BuyLinks from "../components/BuyLinks.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the Book"
  description="What The Day After covers: agent-readiness, legibility, and the operating model for restructuring software companies around AI agents."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About the book</p>
      <h1>The Day After AI</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        How to Restructure Your Software Company for the Age of AI Agents.
      </p>

      <!-- TODO: replace with the real back-cover synopsis -->
      <p>
        AI coding agents are no longer a novelty &mdash; they read code, propose
        changes, and increasingly ship them. Most organizations weren't built
        for that. Decisions live in Slack threads, architecture lives in one
        engineer's head, and "how this system actually works" is tribal
        knowledge instead of something a machine (or a new hire) can read.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>The Day After AI</em> argues that the companies who benefit most from
        AI agents won't be the ones who buy the best tools &mdash; they'll be the
        ones who make their systems <strong>legible</strong>: navigable,
        contract-driven, and safe for both agents and humans to act on. The book
        lays out the operating model for that shift, from assessing where a
        codebase stands today to prioritizing what to fix first and how to keep
        it trustworthy as it evolves.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Engineering leaders deciding how much autonomy to hand to AI agents.</li>
        <li>Architects who need a shared vocabulary for "agent-ready" systems.</li>
        <li>Teams already using coding agents who want fewer surprises in production.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Table of contents</h2>
      <!-- TODO: replace with the real chapter list -->
      <ol class="toc">
        <li>Why "the day after" matters</li>
        <li>What makes a codebase legible</li>
        <li>Assessing agent-readiness</li>
        <li>Prioritizing what to extract first</li>
        <li>Writing contracts agents can trust</li>
        <li>Restructuring teams around agentic execution</li>
        <li>Keeping the system honest as it changes</li>
      </ol>

      <BuyLinks />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .toc {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
